ue": "[68] J. F. Clauser and S. Li, Phys. Rev. A 50, 2430 (1994)."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2159794"}}, {"reference": {"label": "69", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "379", "artid": "257", "year": 2003, "page_start": "257", "journal_title": "Phys.Rept."}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Bassi, A."}, {"full_name": "Ghirardi, G."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "source": "arXiv", "value": "[69] A. Bassi and G. Ghirardi, Phys. Rep. 379, 257 (2003)."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/616579"}}, {"reference": {"label": "70", "misc": ["To observe the asymmetric Talbot-Lau effect, at L2 = κL1 with κ > 0, a period of d1 = (κ + 1) /κ × d/r is needed in the first grating. Analogously to the symmetric case (19) a density pattern emerges at z = L1 +L2 which has now a period of κd1. The case κ < 1 is called the fractional Talbot-Lau effect, and it is easily incorporated into the present framework"]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "source": "arXiv", "value": "[70] To observe the asymmetric Talbot-Lau effect, at L2 = κL1 with κ > 0, a period of d1 = (κ + 1) /κ × d/r is needed in the first grating. Analogously to the symmetric case (19) a density pattern emerges at z = L1 +L2 which has now a period of κd1. The case κ < 1 is called the fractional Talbot-Lau effect, and it is easily incorporated into the present framework."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "71", "misc": ["In order to specify the proportionality factor in (19) a normalizable momentum distribution would be needed in the initial Wigner function"]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "source": "arXiv", "value": "[71] In order to specify the proportionality factor in (19) a normalizable momentum distribution would be needed in the initial Wigner function."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "72", "misc": ["Equations (23) and (31) assume that the transmission signal is extremal at xs = 0 and xs = d1/2 which is the case for realistic transmission functions with t(x) = 